{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And they carried it on a pole, by two.</b> What does 'by two' tell us? By two poles. How so? Eight carried the cluster, one carried the fig, and one carried the pomegranate; Joshua and Caleb carried nothing at all, for their entire intent was to bring out an evil report — just as its fruits are extraordinary, so its people is extraordinary. If you wish to know how much the burden of each of them was, go out and learn from the stones that they set up at Gilgal, which they carried upon themselves, each man one stone from the Jordan upon his shoulder, and the Sages weighed them: the weight of each was forty se'ah. And it is a tradition that a load a man carries upon his shoulders is only a third of the burden he carries when others help him lift it. This is the language of Rashi, of blessed memory." "<b>And I saw</b> in the Midrash: 'And they carried it on a pole, by two' — these are Joshua and Caleb. When attached it is called a vine-branch (זמורה), and when detached they called it a pole (מוט). And none of the ten among them had the strength to carry that pole, but Joshua and Caleb carried it by themselves. Therefore 'and they carried it on a pole, by two,' and this was a hint to them that they were worthy to enter the land and to have an inheritance in it."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:32:03.646574095Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}